<h2>What Could be Improved</h2>
<p>A few extras, if you want to practice some Elisp:</p>
<ul>
<li>Replace the index and <code>mod</code> with Emacs' built-in <code>ring.el</code>.</li>
<li>Show the visible columns and lines in the header, so you know how
much fits on screen with each font and size.</li>
<li>Start cycling from the font you are currently using.</li>
<li>Add a key that restores the font and size you had before opening the
menu.</li>
<li>Cycle themes, or a second list for the <code>variable-pitch</code> face, the
same way.</li>
<li>Turn it into a publishable package: <code>defcustom</code>s, an autoload,
proper headers, and a clean <code>checkdoc</code> and byte-compile.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Honorable Mentions</h2>
<p>Font picking seems to be in the air lately. If you'd rather use
something ready-made, or just want to see other takes on the idea,
check these out:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://elpa.gnu.org/packages/show-font.html">show-font</a>, by
Protesilaos, on GNU ELPA: previews installed fonts in a dedicated
buffer, and <code>show-font-tabulated</code> lists every font rendered in its
own typeface.</li>
<li><a href="https://gitlab.com/vegasharmon/flick">flick</a>, "a quick and simple
font picker", by Vegas Harmon, recently shared on r/emacs.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/emacs/comments/1w31p3u/comment/p6wxkuc/">This r/emacs comment</a>,
from a recent discussion on the same subject.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Wrapping Up</h2>
<p>There's nothing specifically clever here: a list, an index, a <code>mod</code>,
and a transient menu holding it all together.</p>
<p>I hope this educational post helps you with the basics of using
transient, and maybe teaches you something new about Emacs Lisp along
the way.</p>
<p>As for me, I'm currently on font 7 of 16. Ask me again next month.</p>
